// Introduction Adventures in Technophilosophy
// Part I 
// Virtual Worlds
title: Virtual_Worlds
---
Chalmers: AR, VR technology is progressing rapidly. I am sure you're aware.
Player: Well, yes I guess I am.
Chalmers: You are aware that these systems offer worlds that are partly virtual and partly physical?
Player: I guess, but regarding VR worlds, are these fully virtual?
Chalmers: Depends. What about your interactions with these worlds?
Player: Okay I see your point.
Chalmers: Anyway, do you suspect, as do I, that in a century's time these virtual worlds will be indistinguishable from the real one?
Player: Depends on what you mean by "indistinguishable", but sure, I will grant you that it is highly probably that virtual worlds will become "indistinguishable" from the real one.
Chalmers: These worlds will then become occupied by simulated people then will they not?
Player: Perhaps not necessarily, but I want to see where you're going with this argument, so I will conditionally grant you the point.
Chalmers: An the simulated people will have simulated brains, and bodies and such? Including simulated life processes such as aging, death, and birth?
Player: Okay, sure, why not? A bit far fetched if you ask me.
Chalmers: And will the simulated people know that they are in a simulated world?
Player: How should I know?
Chalmers: Well can you know if you are in a simulation?
Player: I guess you've got me.
===

